          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.415,
            0.891,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "For each point, the value of the y-coordinate is three times that of the x-coordinate. We can therefore say that y = 3x."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.455,
            0.891,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": "**Example 13:** Let us plot the points (– 3, 6), (– 2, 4), (0, 0), (1, – 2), (2, – 4), (3, – 6) in the coordinate plane on a graph paper as shown in Fig. 2.7. Join the points (– 3, 6) and (3, – 6) using a ruler. Doing so, observe that all five points lie on a straight line. Can you guess the equation of this line by looking at the relationship between the x and y coordinates of each point?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.556,
            0.797,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.46,
